{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a powerful nobleman who ruled the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. He served as regent for the Kingdom of Jerusalem at times when kings were minors or incapacitated. His life and career were defined by complex political maneuvering within the crusader states. Raymond was born into a prominent family with strong ties to the Latin East. His father was Raymond II who was assassinated by the Hashashin sect. This event forced the young Raymond to assume leadership of his domain at a very early age. He faced numerous challenges from internal rivals and external threats throughout his long tenure.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond inherited the title of count after his father died in 1152. He was only a child at the time so his mother acted as regent initially. The Assassins had killed his father because of an alleged plot against their order. Raymond spent his youth consolidating power within Tripoli while navigating the dangerous politics of the region. He eventually came of age and took full control of his county. His diplomatic skills allowed him to maintain stability despite constant pressure from Muslim neighbors.\n\n*Regency and Political Influence*\nRaymond became one of the most influential figures in the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He served as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. This role placed him at the center of court politics and military strategy. Raymond often clashed with other barons who sought to gain more power for themselves. He supported the faction led by Raymond of Antioch against the rival faction of Guy of Lusignan. His influence waned when Baldwin IV became too ill to rule effectively. The court split into two opposing camps that fought for control of the throne.\n\n*Later Years and Death*\nRaymond retired to his own lands after losing his position as regent. He died in 1187 shortly before the Battle of Hattin took place. His death occurred while he was suffering from an illness that weakened him significantly. The fall of Jerusalem happened soon after his passing. His legacy remains complicated due to his shifting alliances and controversial decisions during his life. Historians debate whether he acted in the best interest of the crusader states or his own personal gain. The County of Tripoli continued under his descendants after his death. His story illustrates the fragile nature of power in the Latin East during the crusades. The region faced constant warfare and internal strife that eventually led to its decline. Raymond III remains a significant figure in the history of the crusader states.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0022097188}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0033115083}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.06942511}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0062119984}, {"token": " powerful", "logprob": -0.821722}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.010272754}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.31898597}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.019931644}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.10319209}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.025532383}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.00057582743}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.00529874}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.02773656}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.009590633}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -1.9312092e-05}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -3.576285e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.050698254}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.2520674}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -1.2673998}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -5.3764834e-05}, {"token": " reg", "logprob": -0.18950726}, {"token": "ent", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -2.7537724e-05}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.22938652}, {"token": " Kingdom", "logprob": -0.26220